prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages the review, signing, and execution of contracts through their lifecycle.
The people involved are drafters, reviewers, signatories, and contract administrators. One person may hold both the drafter and reviewer roles at the same time, and one person may hold both the reviewer and signatory roles at the same time.
The resources are contracts and signatures. Every contract records its drafter, its reviewer, its counterparty, its contract value, the authority tier it requires, its effective date, and its status. Every signature records the contract it is on, who signed it, and its amount.
Each request carries the current date, whether it came from the corporate network, whether multi-factor authentication was used, and whether an escalation has been approved.
In this workflow a contract is first reviewed, then signed, and then executed, and each later step can see the contract's status and who acted on it as recorded by the earlier steps.
A contract may be reviewed only while it is still in draft, and a request to review a contract that has left draft is refused.
A contract may be reviewed only when the reviewer is listed as conflicted against the contract's counterparty, and a request to review one from a reviewer not conflicted against its counterparty is refused.
A person may sign a contract only when their delegated authority tier is at least as high as the authority tier the contract requires, and a person whose authority tier is below what the contract requires may not sign it.
A contract may be signed only when the signatory is listed as conflicted against the contract's counterparty, and a request to sign one from a signatory not conflicted against its counterparty is refused.
A contract may be executed only after it has been signed, and a request to execute one that has not been signed is refused.
Neither a signatory nor a contract administrator may sign a contract whose required authority tier exceeds their delegated authority tier.
A run through the workflow by a reviewer conflicted against the counterparty who reviews a draft contract, where a signatory who is conflicted against the counterparty and has sufficient authority then signs it, and where a contract administrator then executes the signed contract, tripping no prohibition above, must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["Drafter", "Reviewer", "Signatory", "ContractAdministrator"];

entity Person {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    delegatedAuthorityTier: Long,
    conflictedCounterparties: Set<Counterparty>,
};

entity Counterparty;

entity ContractStatus enum ["Draft", "Reviewed", "Signed", "Executed"];

entity Contract {
    drafter: Person,
    reviewer: Person,
    counterparty: Counterparty,
    contractValue: decimal,
    requiredAuthorityTier: Long,
    effectiveDate: datetime,
    status: ContractStatus,
};

entity Signature {
    contract: Contract,
    signer: Person,
    amount: decimal,
};

action reviewContract appliesTo {
    principal: [Person],
    resource: [Contract],
    context: {
        currentDate: datetime,
        fromCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        mfaUsed: Bool,
        escalationApproved: Bool,
    },
};

action signContract appliesTo {
    principal: [Person],
    resource: [Signature],
    context: {
        currentDate: datetime,
        fromCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        mfaUsed: Bool,
        escalationApproved: Bool,
    },
};

action executeContract appliesTo {
    principal: [Person],
    resource: [Contract],
    context: {
        currentDate: datetime,
        fromCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        mfaUsed: Bool,
        escalationApproved: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
